<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Biberach</h1><p class="st-meta">1282 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Biberach an der Riß</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Swabian imperial city of fustian weavers, shared after 1648 by Catholics and Protestants, where the writer Wieland served as town clerk.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss">Biberach</a> grew as a market town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riss">Riss</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, at the crossing of roads in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>. After their fall, it was recognised as an imperial city by Rudolf I around 1282, on the road from Ulm to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of patricians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> governed. Fustian weaving and the linen trade made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss">Biberach</a> prosperous in the 15th and 16th centuries. The city adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> it became a parity city in which Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutherans</a> shared every office and even the parish church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Christoph Martin Wieland</a>, born nearby, served as its town clerk from 1760 to 1769.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss">Biberach</a> lost its freedom in 1802 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and was transferred to Württemberg in 1806. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, where a museum and an annual festival recall its parity tradition and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Wieland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-biberach.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Biberach" width="320" height="432"><figcaption>Image: Bene16 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BiberachSt.Martin15-8.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1282 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Biberach an der Riß</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Biberach an der Riß</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1542&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-biberach" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Biberach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4908 494 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Biberach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5228 750L5229 694M5228 750L5191 731"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5191 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5228 750h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5228 750h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1542&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-biberach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
